    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "A major challenge in holographic approaches to quantum gravity has been to construct a dictionary mapping boundary observables to bulk observables. Hamilton, Kabat, Lifschytz and Low (HKLL) developed a scheme for computing CFT operators dual to local bulk fields in the semi-classical limit of the bulk theory, based on knowledge of the classical bulk equations of motion. X Qi and T Nebabu generalized the HKLL protocol to createe a protocol which constructs bulk operators in 1+1 spacetime based on boundary operators from generalized free field theories. This protocol solely relies on boundary data (unlike HKLL) and paplies to a class of theories more general than conformal field theories. However, this protocol relies on discretizing the bulk spacetime to numerically compute operators on a set of lattice points. This thesis, which constitutes the beginning part of an ongoing research project with Xiaoliang Qi, Tamra Nebabu, and Jean Wang, we explore ways of analytically extending this protocol to apply to arbitrary points in a continuous bulk spacetime. The desired end result of this overall project will provide us with a powerful method to construct arbitrary holographic 1+1 quantum gravity theories from a very general class of quantum theories, advancing our understanding of lower dimensional quantum gravity. This thesis covers numerical studies and analytical work which have been done to create this protocol, and canvassing current challenges which still need to be surmounted in order to have a full continuous protocol.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Kemp, E. (2024). Investigations into a Continuous Protocol for Bulk Reconstruction of Generalized Free Fields. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/wq443nv2994. https://doi.org/10.25740/wq443nv2994.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
